course: dictionary
lesson: 4392
title: Hair: one word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The word hair can mean the strands that grow on your head, or the fur on an animal. Context tells you which one. «Brush your hair before school» is about your head, but «The hairs of some caterpillars cause rashes» is about animals.
explain: Hair is a noun that does not usually have a plural, so we say «grey hair», not «greys hairs». One single strand is called a hair. When you count strands, you say «a hair» or «three hairs».
explain: Hair has a few useful phrases. «By a hair» means only just, by a tiny amount. «Let your hair down» means relax and enjoy yourself. A «hair-raising» story is so scary it makes you feel shivers.
fact: Hair is what grows on your head and on parts of your body.
fact: A hair is one single thin strand.
fact: Hair, or fur, also grows on many animals, such as foxes and rabbits.
fact: «By a hair» means only just: «She won the race by a hair.»
fact: «Hair-raising» means very scary or exciting.
exercise: Which word means one single thin strand? | Fur | *A hair | Hairy | Haircut
exercise: «The hairs of some caterpillars cause rashes, so you do not touch hairy caterpillars.» What does hairy mean here? | Smooth and shiny | *Covered in hair or fur | Very long and tangled | Bright red
exercise: Ellie says, «I won the race by a hair.» What does this mean? | *She won only just, by a tiny amount | She pulled someone's hair | She lost her hair in the race | She won easily, with lots of hair
test: Rangers study droppings to learn which animals live in a place. Which droppings often hold fur and seeds? | *Fox | Deer | Bird | Rabbit
test: Everyone gets older: «grey ___», glasses and stiffer knees come. Which word fits the gap? | hairy | hairs | *hair | a hair
exam: Grandad says, «Let your hair down this weekend.» What does he mean? | Brush your hair neatly | Dye your hair red | Cut your hair short | *Relax and have fun
